e dropping upward upwards  of 2,000 shells a day on them I  understood why they would go out and  literally man trenches to try and keep  the Serbs back that did not save the  residents of Sarajevo from the  pernicious effects of war in that you  know those people who had a penchant for  violence rose to positions of power the  gangster class organized the first  militias and when they weren't Manning  the barricades they were usually off  looting the apartments of ethnic Serbs  who lived in the city and oftentimes  murdering them so it doesn't save you  from the poison of war but there are  times when you are faced with your own  physical destruction the destruction of  your family the destruction of your  community the destruction of your nation  and I think at that moment it I don't  like to turn just war but war becomes  inevitable and and and and anybody I  think at least in moral terms it's  understandable as to why they would use  violence to try and protect themselves  and protect those around them but that  does not save them in the end of course  from the the evil consequences of war  and the damage that war does to them as  individuals and to the society at large  thank you hi hi my name is Chelsea  Peterson from McLean High School and you  talked about how Wars become a form of  entertainment in our country especially  and how it has penetrated the media and  books music and popular movies and  everything and I was just wondering if  you think that the media has changed our  connotation of war in the American  soldier and whether the necessity for  war in the media will continue to  perpetuate over time or whether it will  eventually dissipate  I think that you know when you go back  and look at you know even all the way  back to the ancient Greeks or you know  you know when you read the Aeneid that  myth that warrior myth is just a really  powerful myth within almost all cultures  especially Imperial cultures and I think  what's  different is the way we would  disseminate the myth but the myth has  always been there and I think will  always be there you know there's no  shortage of great memoirs by people who  have been through combat and have come  back and written about the reality of  war but it's painful it's painful to  read it's painful to hear it's painful  to understand and I think when you talk  to people who come back even you know  these soldiers and Marines are coming  back from Iraq they'll sit around and  people will ask them how it was and and  if they really start to tell them it  most people begin to get really  uncomfortable really quickly the fact is  a lot of people don't want to hear the  only people who really understand war  are the people on the ground who see it  because the images however disturbing  they may be are so sanitized and so in  and and of course the ones that begin to  to reflect any kind of truth so  infrequent that we're spared from seeing  how horrible war is and when people come  back and try and and lay it out for us  it's painful to hear people prefer to go  back to the myth there's a kind of  natural gravitation back towards the  myth this is I'm trying to figure out  the gravitational pull for the press  assuming let's assume let's give benefit  of the doubt that this is not purposeful  propagandizing is is the common thread  here the narrative the need of a  narrative you have mythology the main  vehicle for dispersing mythology is  storytelling the main vehicle for  journalism is storytelling right is it  looking at this chaotic battle in front  of you and needing to impose some  narrative thread onto it that well you  know that's a good point but you know in  a because when you're actually in a  situation of combat the the most all the  time you're just completely confused  you're desperately trying to figure out  where the firing is coming from it's  often coming from you know more than one  place or your friends friendly fire yeah  and and and you only see a tiny little  you know there's a small perimeter of  stuff that you could you're actually  seen I mean the landscape of  you know even a firefight is beyond at  the moment your ability to comprehend  and grasp so what you do when when  fighting ends is immediately try and  create a narrative to make sense of it  and that in many ways is what the press  does for you it takes what for the  individual is completely confusing and  makes a narrative well the press knows  very well and and I and I think it's  probably not always conscience conscious  but they they they understand in a kind  of reflexive way what the best narrative  is and that is you know who was heroic  where are those you know refugees or  people who were repressed who have now  been liberated by our you know heroic  troops and and what are the perfidious  crimes of the enemy that fits into a  very comfortable mythic war narrative  that allows that is the framework by  which many of them seek to understand  what's happening but more importantly  it's that framework that the people at  home expect hi please hi I'm Sam  headland from the Center for Strategic  and International Studies it seems to me  like your work on trying to find some  kind of middle ground in how to go  forward in more if we find ourselves in  war without being seduced by it and  corrupted by it for I you could take  exception to this but I see a certain  commonality between that and certain  realist thinkers like Reinhold Niebuhr  and so I'm just wondering what utility  do you see in philosophical reflection  on war and what where do you think that  could take us Sam terrific question and  I'm sorry to do this to you but about 30  seconds for an answer  Reinhold Niebuhr Czar great of course  influence I mean you were right to cite  him I think that we want to look at  people who understand human reality  always be wary of utopianist  and cynics and  understand as Niebuhr said the decision  in life is never between moral and  immoral but usually between immoral and  more immoral and once you realize that  it becomes a lot easier to make choices  okay thank you gentlemen  sorry to rush your and your answer these  are a couple of the books from Chris  Hedges  about war interesting reads we would  highly recommend them I want to thank  you for your questions and comments  throughout the discussion I particularly  want to thank Becky Sipos and her  students from MacLean high school and  also Dan Gregory and his well students  of life I guess you call them employees  at CSIS thanks to all of you for joining  us next week when we return to the  Newseum our discussion will be about  reporting on religion that's our version  of a holiday program I guess hope you'll  join us then until then for all of us at  the Freedom Forum Museum and close-up  foundation I'm John molesky thanks for  watching
 

